{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:19.703954Z","id":"3388a264d5bd2e55dffef793f313b0da8f5ddf14d52b439980201fadaa024534","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Fleet::open logs when an instance's setup secret is absent ('/setup stays closed; add it to the Secret and restart the pods'); docs/kubernetes.md and deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml say adding or rotating a secret needs kubectl rollout restart. Reading secrets from mounted files is not done. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"2f6f4aabb1af8bf9e0ea28b9a7d85192b524cf4b7e20848561fa0df61292f747","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:19.782303Z","id":"9d0f4c5c536431dea65f0572ca2ba4d8b682651f14cf7d0302bcab3cf7bd5776","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"FleetConfig::db_limits opens an instance's database with clax_pagevfs::open_with; --db-max-transaction-mib (CLAX_FLEET_DB_MAX_TRANSACTION_MIB) defaults to 256 MiB; docs/kubernetes.md, Sizing, names it. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"440a39279e3d28ace5ceedf2f179d3cc04e4a840b0157db55daa5f8684df3f5b","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:19.860587Z","id":"62a500fa3316be8e328addf94d50e9956de3630e1e5d668a7a0a64ec09a13dfe","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Fleet::stop runs the core's stop on a task of its own and waits at most STOP_TIMEOUT; the slot stays stopping, and Fleet::start refuses it, until that task ends, so a store left running is never opened twice. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"2b58ea4e68c69e4e0e2876bb0b20c29b6a2dc4020ef22861f3aa473a969a21b9","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:19.939910Z","id":"c7c2c4796bf1fc4c11f43ccd2ff0c4522435e69e2ee045f0bc7984c9fb3a9719","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Fleet::renew_all renews every held lease in one Coordinator::renew_all statement before any stop; renewals and reload no longer wait on a slot a start holds (try_lock); a tick's stops are each bounded by STOP_TIMEOUT. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"23f0a975e228cb86b5b0595803ec2f3c230d43424ccee7bed28ee7480b4785dd","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.018524Z","id":"f6988c9a748757722e0a63f54f8fc2a0396f234abbda99640db79c845f78b6de","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Fleet::reload logs a failed reload once per distinct error; /status shows the catalog's loaded_at and error, and /metrics clax_fleet_catalog_stale; Fleet::new's error says the other processes keep the catalog in effect; check-catalog --sqlite reads shards from the catalog, and docs/kubernetes.md shows running it from the image. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"96fb105339b7fdee5422bf5abc5a22302cdc25adf3d3bf50af238a7c642c6b6c","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.097929Z","id":"d54710c67005215042734c9983402da6c6352d8e3742e5d693839ee36faaa5e0","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"On SQLite shards a pod starts an instance only when its home is there or its entry says create = true (Fleet::open); test shards::a_shard_without_the_home_does_not_start_an_empty_instance passes. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"d2e4bf1c2d28a1caca2c6dd74b26ad6bda15aeab22782d34ee90985891c9be79","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.176153Z","id":"d53b3d456aca67ea75e2b6d4f6335e1a1273338c79fbf8fa77288d3f2536a7e4","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Fleet::start checks draining after taking the slot's lock, so a start either sees the drain or is stopped by it; drain stops every slot. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"6522bc922e83f96d47389641803064670b1afa891e71c2492ab2550aafa8ba80","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.254203Z","id":"8edb416849989190046266d95816b67d87b138190bdf095c96db2690792a5f36","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"The admin port serves /metrics in Prometheus text: requests local/forwarded/refused, forward errors, starts and failures, stops, lease losses and renewal failures, handovers, catalog failures, heartbeat failures, and gauges of running instances, catalog size, staleness and readiness (Fleet::metrics). Pool waits are not counted. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"3e57c16e8984015b4fd72e778b6a339e1820c8c5286cb9c86b51dfc45f1352f4","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.332519Z","id":"8d485b735c13738d42cfab662fa3203375b7c464b93e7afb1f122cdb32d5ae5a","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"/status and /metrics answer only a loopback peer or the peer key (Fleet::admin_router, allowed); /healthz and /readyz stay open for probes; base/networkpolicy.yaml limits 7481. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"7fed13558708928705b3c596bb215826a027ba89423b5ef01a4f59ba2a3232e2","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.410943Z","id":"a47df3f4ed93584a0748289b36a05dc084dc4eefa351a28b6a37e451ac78879b","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"dispatch believes x-clax-fleet-hop and x-clax-fleet-instance only beside x-clax-fleet-key matching the fleet's peer key (constant-time, proxy::key_matches); test leases::a_clients_hop_headers_are_ignored_without_the_peer_key passes. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"462bbaa8851ae571b3e05b01e3960265e7fe21a46f1d62b1966d5f5f9e3d5251","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.490423Z","id":"a7cce4789937b2bcc75e8a2e58e0791d559a3d4acd7034938c9e20dcd2785875","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"CLAX_POSTGRES_POOL defaults to 8, the coordinator has its own 2; docs/kubernetes.md, Connections, gives pods x (pool + 2) against max_connections and PgBouncer; postgres-dev sets max_connections=200. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"16a32d00d46e3cee91159c8cd1634003a6feae5be12cfaeb18cbd90f1b950b42","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.568228Z","id":"0b2c67fb53f2a4efe5a35fb225d27ace41f9c51b8c9ecb391231254f832462db","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"CLAX_FLEET_LOG_FORMAT=json logs JSON lines (tracing-subscriber json); each request runs in an `instance` span (Fleet::handle); RUST_LOG is in docs/kubernetes.md's table. An instance's background tasks are not in a span. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"74d56d45b73b58523464374a4d8ea5d04e46060b0c2ae90c014fbe24a979dd5d","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.647483Z","id":"f749d8c340bf400b3a6c317317db86bd17c552cc28b18e8ade5c199d969f82c0","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"PgCoordinator gets a pool of its own (main.rs COORD_POOL, 2 connections), so heartbeats, renewals and lease lookups never queue behind page and content jobs. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"4a8bd70e5ad33414979db0b47aaf55f53804f11c4b70b34cbee3f950d0249db9","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.725496Z","id":"f1f04c7e40298b2e29167d6a53bf719c9fa0ef6f563250234c9019b5ff21f139","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"pages.rs Cache: bounded on every insert with FIFO eviction, nothing inserted on commit (written chunks are dropped), read-ahead only on a sequential miss, keys (file number, index) without allocating; tests the_cache_keeps_its_bound_dropping_the_oldest and forgetting_keeps_other_files_and_the_order_in_step pass. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"72fddb3e265311fc8351f26ab18838618696cd1f564dbab122457d302467e060","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.804134Z","id":"bed0947144a078360c256e0ee3eb208f06ee98ed849ad887dd2eba4698d3b605","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Every statement in pages.rs, content.rs, coord.rs and Fence::check is typed (query_typed/execute_typed: one round trip, no prepare); a commit skips the size upsert when unchanged and the delete unless it truncates (Commit::truncates). `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"360bc30d6c6375edaecb384ee5303549f19039313f901b15359113af070e7910","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.882910Z","id":"55a338f961b61b650a377f0aed51144452cc8038a9c63cbf7fc7263e8a0503a3","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"PageStore::commit takes a Commit holding the dirty set in an Arc (shared, not cloned); chunks are sent WRITE_BATCH (256) at a time, bounding the encoded statement to 1 MiB; committed chunks are not put in the read cache. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"d31dddc1abb2e909654a38d59fb654c79e1ac74e3a84257054f6102e03e41e04","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.961805Z","id":"b0b71c4615b131aecb55106b409b3f4d66efc92f62f1f01692cdb75b0c49d81f","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Same fix as 72fddb3e26: the cache is bounded on every insert and commits insert nothing; tests in crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"717de9f5b4ffedb51b90ce5de4a9ddeab387323352293f821463c814292426cf","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:21.040663Z","id":"b8b02254db5c7f7cdd34a56d908d131071799c217fb5f487248d35499cac1403","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"pg::config sets SslMode::Require when TLS is on and refuses sslmode=disable, and refuses sslmode=require with TLS off; test tls_on_never_falls_back_to_plain_text in tests/postgres.rs passes. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"720cc33ec50693405ccf24defbe28d6edb5a6ca9ebba086e4a8586c1e791d2b4","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:21.119917Z","id":"7f67ad8e614a95cf4c7eb449bee7e9e6a42343d4083e10563dc7a40e1cc4bcbe","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"pg::config gives every connection a 10 s connect timeout, TCP keepalives after 30 s idle and a 120 s statement_timeout, so a call to a server lost without a reset fails.","supersedes":"869880ef72dacf9464d361c4f0453f9332deada5ef0ed6e4cb49dec0b990621f","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:21.198513Z","id":"290446e52dc661453b9759b3843d9e949ed8ae644ecbc346eb7eca81cd714c80","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"7e166a342dfa9b407a824758c5b3af100cc1bbfa51a428d973ce8853132868d2","summary":"git:347050368 chose without measuring: schema version 2 (crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs SCHEMA_2) sets clax_fleet_chunks.bytes STORAGE MAIN and autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor 0.02. No measurement was possible (no PostgreSQL server); measure with a realistic instance before relying on it.","tags":["session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:21.198808Z","id":"2ac7070bb1b2634d29a6878072dc389ac5af260825c5e49a36658577658865b9","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"proxy::Proxy forwards over a hyper-util pooled client with keep-alive (one pool per owner, 64 idle), resolving names only for new connections; upgrades alone take a connection of their own; proxy tests pass. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"4e75db7e1ba8a4bbbc4c5d7abc3c97765776cd8d2d72e0a91aaa627b0a6d4a2d","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:21.277895Z","id":"21f28cb62f4804e04d12a66331bc2446927f8ea485f818f9c4ad31092c57ccca","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md, Security, says forwards are plain HTTP and to run an encrypted pod network or mTLS mesh; base/networkpolicy.yaml limits who reaches the ports; proxy.rs module docs say so. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"fc451012515ec2bacae0917337b16d7e031bee0b11685cd30d32faee86be587a","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:52.427071Z","id":"922988b532974b7741e44fa7d86ae06fcabb139ea7b1ad11a8ab1ca5a8eedfeb","body":{"detail":"Proxy::new sets only a connect timeout (CONNECT_TIMEOUT, 5 s) and an idle timeout on hyper_util's legacy Client; it sets no response-header timeout and no HttpConnector::set_keepalive. forward() awaits proxy.pool.request(...) (proxy.rs:190) and, for an upgrade, the handshake and send_request (proxy.rs:209-223) with no bound. When an owner's node is lost or partitioned (no FIN, no RST), a request written to a kept-alive pooled connection (reused for up to IDLE_TIMEOUT, 60 s) waits until the kernel gives up retransmitting (about 15 minutes with Linux's default tcp_retries2). Fleet::handle forgets the route only on a 502 or 503 response (fleet.rs:399-407), which never comes, so for ROUTE_CACHE (10 s) every new request for that instance is sent to the dead address too, and the clients' requests and the forwarding pod's tasks pile up although the lease expires after 30 s and another pod could serve the instance.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":123},"end":{"line":135},"content_hash":"906348f656f15e406b6c2528ee0808b8cda18e14d8f9edcf3472c786a108205d"},"suggested_fix":"Set TCP keepalive on the connector (set_keepalive with a short interval) and bound the wait for the owner's response headers (tokio::time::timeout around pool.request and around the upgrade's handshake and send_request, generous enough for Clax's long-poll endpoints), answering 502 and forgetting the route when it passes; event streams stay unbounded because only the headers are timed. Add a test with an owner that accepts and never answers.","summary":"A forward has no timeout past connecting, and pooled connections have no TCP keepalive, so requests sent to an owner whose node vanished hang for minutes","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","performance","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:52.427154Z","id":"d0fd8aa8d5046d0f7ec4884036d4d817bad45041925e390d296cf55c401853e0","body":{"detail":"admin_router's allowed() admits a caller when peer.ip().to_canonical().is_loopback(), meaning 127.0.0.0/8 or ::1. docs/kubernetes.md (Security, Pod network) recommends running the fleet on a mesh with mTLS. With Istio's sidecar in its default REDIRECT interception, the proxy connects to the application from 127.0.0.6 (https://istio.io/latest/blog/2021/upcoming-networking-changes/), so every connection to port 7481, from any pod the mesh and base/networkpolicy.yaml admit, arrives from a loopback address and passes the check, and the peer key is never needed. The docs' promise that /status and /metrics answer only to the pod's loopback or the peer key (docs/kubernetes.md, What clients see) then does not hold.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1083},"end":{"line":1087},"content_hash":"75e31f61d759a6826e5eeee5e0955ed8017e6cfd733e8bbba6bc2f5d4248192d"},"suggested_fix":"Bind a separate loopback-only listener for the unauthenticated admin view (or require the peer key or a dedicated admin token on 7481 always), instead of inferring trust from the peer address; at least document that a sidecar mesh defeats the loopback check and how to exclude port 7481 from interception (traffic.sidecar.istio.io/excludeInboundPorts).","summary":"/status and /metrics trust any caller whose address is loopback, which under a sidecar mesh is every caller","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:52.427199Z","id":"5a51b34f14988b29ceb439b2ca6f73795c95da296aea341876dec4b27d41424a","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml admits port 7481 from namespaces labelled clax.empathic.dev/metrics=true for a Prometheus that scrapes /metrics, but the /metrics handler answers 403 to any non-loopback caller without x-clax-fleet-key. docs/kubernetes.md (Operating it) does not say how a scraper authenticates, and a Prometheus ServiceMonitor or scrape config sets an Authorization header easily but a custom header only in recent versions. The only key accepted is the fleet's peer key (Fleet::is_peer), the same secret that makes dispatch (fleet.rs:1204-1219) believe x-clax-fleet-hop and x-clax-fleet-instance on port 7480, so handing it to the monitoring stack widens who can pass for a fleet process.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1113},"end":{"line":1129},"content_hash":"842d40f5450468694c44f18caae99375adbd1e62ecff18467eff7548f3d578aa"},"suggested_fix":"Accept a separate read-only metrics credential (for example CLAX_FLEET_METRICS_TOKEN as an Authorization: Bearer token), or serve /metrics without authentication since it carries counters only and the NetworkPolicy already limits who reaches 7481; document the scrape configuration in docs/kubernetes.md.","summary":"A Prometheus the NetworkPolicy admits to /metrics gets 403 unless it is given the peer key, which also makes it a trusted forwarder on 7480","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","devops","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:52.427236Z","id":"0d1e30d07e119b8d1310bddf6ab9ece529eaaf8d17fddbc50b63052f7be67d39","body":{"detail":"Fleet::start records nothing when open() fails: it logs at error level, releases the lease and answers 503, and the next request for the instance runs the whole start again. On PostgreSQL each attempt bumps the lease epoch (coord.rs acquire), runs pg::claim_origin, opens the page VFS and reruns migrations (fleet.rs:684-698); on SQLite shards a home missing on its shard (fleet.rs:613-619) or a KEK mismatch (place_kek) fails the same way each time. Requests wait on the slot's lookup and running locks with no bound on how many queue, so an instance with a persistent fault (a failing migration, a wrong CLAX_KEK) turns any client's retries into a steady stream of database work and one error line per request.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":592},"end":{"line":601},"content_hash":"855a94742ddad0b101192337ad4c8ba6044dc371b649786c8f901500ad460e76"},"suggested_fix":"Remember a failed start on the slot (when, and the error) and answer 503 without retrying for a backoff that grows per failure (a few seconds up to a minute), reset on success or on a catalog change for that instance; expose the failure in /status. Add a test that two requests within the backoff make one start attempt.","summary":"An instance that fails to start is started again on every request, with no backoff, so anonymous traffic drives repeated lease takes, database opens and error logs","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","performance","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:52.427269Z","id":"42b00a21d3ce34ff74eddb0165d7a3e3360b3a3ff48b69479022814cafb29674","body":{"detail":"Each instance's [hosted] setup_secret_env names a variable in the one process environment all instances share (fleet.rs open, hosted.setup_open). Catalog::parse checks names, origins and site domains for repeats but not setup_secret_env, and when a config omits it the default is CLAX_SETUP_SECRET for every instance (crates/clax-core/src/config.rs:1291). A catalog with two instances that both omit setup_secret_env, or name the same variable, gives both /setup the same secret: the operator who hands tenant A its setup secret has also handed A the means to take over tenant B's instance before B's owner sets it up. docs/kubernetes.md's example names CLAX_SETUP_SECRET_ACME, but nothing enforces distinct names.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":196},"end":{"line":209},"content_hash":"6453fcc49c6cac0a1ba180cf5c3700bd80cd24147fafcee686d6a643eeccb030"},"suggested_fix":"In Catalog::parse, refuse two instances whose effective setup_secret_env (after the default) is the same, naming both instances; consider also refusing the fleet's own variables (CLAX_KEK, CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY, the database URL) as a setup_secret_env. Add the case to a_bad_catalog_is_refused_naming_what_is_wrong.","summary":"The catalog lets several instances read the same setup secret, so whoever holds one tenant's setup secret can claim another tenant's unclaimed instance","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:52.427298Z","id":"4d874951d338e2426f9920c4f949d84cf672a68b5de1e4e14efae99c6e20e60d","body":{"detail":"The module docs (catalog.rs:26-29) and docs/kubernetes.md (The catalog) say that because no site domain is, is under or is above another instance's hosts, 'each tenant's pages stay cross-site from every other tenant'. The check (at_or_under, label nesting) does not establish that: browsers decide same-site by registrable domain from the Public Suffix List. In the documented example, acme's site domain acme-sites.example.com and any other tenant's origin such as globex.clax.example.com share example.com, so acme's artifact pages are same-site with globex's origin: SameSite cookies do not separate them, and an artifact page can set a Domain=example.com cookie that every tenant's origin receives (docs/hosted/public-suffix.md describes the same risk among artifacts).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":226},"end":{"line":252},"content_hash":"691082016ef4c6d20b9a8e1595a2085f2df5ddf73cf2961929e5f89dbc01bd4c"},"suggested_fix":"Either compare registrable domains (the psl crate) and refuse a site domain that shares one with another instance's public host or site domain unless the site domain is itself a public suffix, or change the docs to state the weaker guarantee (no nesting) and require each tenant's site domain to be its own registrable domain or a PSL entry; change the example so the site domain does not share example.com with the origins.","summary":"The catalog checks that hosts are not nested, but documents that tenants stay cross-site, and its own example puts every tenant in one registrable domain","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:52.427335Z","id":"c5566b466dc866b81afa99d60726c086bb09c67f17f6bf3ce57b3eb5d6d61cd7","body":{"detail":"Every third tick, Fleet::tick pushes a stop for each running instance whose preferred member is another settled pod, and joins them all at once (fleet.rs:802-808). When the autoscaler adds pods (deploy/k8s/postgres/hpa.yaml allows 2 to 10), the instances rendezvous hashing moves to them (half of them when going from 2 to 4) stop on the same tick once the new pods pass coord::SETTLE; their clients reconnect together and every instance cold-starts on the new pods at once (page reads and migration checks against PostgreSQL), while the tick itself waits up to STOP_TIMEOUT for the stops. docs/kubernetes.md (Scaling) slows scale-down for this reason but nothing paces scale-up.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":784},"end":{"line":799},"content_hash":"5a6ce85cb9d814db094562b3148ad577fde3f9ee2061b0bf6a9beb049d20ff0d"},"suggested_fix":"Cap handovers per tick (a constant such as 4 per pod per handover tick, or a fraction of the running instances), leaving the rest for later ticks; count the deferred ones in /metrics.","summary":"A scale-up hands over every instance the new pods are preferred for in the same tick, cutting all their streams at once","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","performance","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:57.772789Z","id":"ad7b26f31a4ee3b39e16beb15aff3f2b51d8f900783c6f0b2deead1435dfaf2d","body":{"detail":"Fleet::status (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs, \"commit\": clax_core::build_commit()) reads a OnceLock that only clax_core::set_build_commit fills; the clax binary fills it through clax-commands (crates/clax-commands/src/lib.rs:72) from crates/clax-cli/build.rs's CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT. clax-fleet has no build script and main() never calls set_build_commit (`grep -rn set_build_commit crates` finds no clax-fleet hit). The image build could not supply one anyway: .dockerignore:6 leaves out .git and deploy/Dockerfile passes no CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT. docs/kubernetes.md:341-343 promises /status gives \"its version and commit\", which an operator needs during a rollout of images tagged by commit (fleet-image.yml's type=sha).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":221},"end":{"line":233},"content_hash":"2f28d44a181fae95b6715bc5a6424844783b3d302ce6a6d828a632bf056026f4"},"suggested_fix":"Give clax-fleet a build.rs that sets CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT as crates/clax-cli/build.rs does and call clax_core::set_build_commit(env!(\"CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT\")) in main; add `ARG CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT` to the build stage and pass `build-args: CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT=${{ github.sha }}` in .github/workflows/fleet-image.yml.","summary":"clax-fleet never records its build commit, so /status always reports \"commit\": \"unknown\"","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:57.772882Z","id":"b0081557f5efda61823d9080d42793e70b1738766fa0849d15382b591b1dc731","body":{"detail":"The loop never checks the response status: a 403 (wrong CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY, fleet.rs admin_router) or a 5xx body fails to parse, `unwrap_or_default` yields Null, and the line reads \"shard 1 (?): ready null, running null\", which looks like a live shard. The hyper client has no connect or request timeout, so a shard whose pod IP drops packets holds the command for the OS connect timeout, shard after shard.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":506},"end":{"line":525},"content_hash":"86744a8a941915e70aa06de9224dccc8338128d76d9e2725b7529fcb614078b2"},"suggested_fix":"Print the HTTP status when it is not 200 (\"refused: 403, check CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY\"), and wrap each request in tokio::time::timeout (clax_fleet::proxy::CONNECT_TIMEOUT is 5 s), querying the shards concurrently.","summary":"`clax-fleet status --backend sqlite` prints a refused or failed shard as \"ready null, running null\" and can hang on an unreachable one","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:57.772907Z","id":"0fac0089023241a46b7c3b14e01f794347f82d0de406cd1e24ae00cf28a964fd","body":{"detail":"The query's `m.seen >= now() - interval '20 seconds'` duplicates coord::MEMBER_TTL (crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs:30). If the TTL changes, status marks live holders as \"(pod not seen)\" or hides dead ones, and the SQL lives outside the coordinator that owns the schema.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":449},"end":{"line":455},"content_hash":"d372f1c703397edbe4b49a6a22f7df45f6519a16b5c5cd8a3616013e92e7b797"},"suggested_fix":"Bind MEMBER_TTL as a parameter (`now() - make_interval(secs => $1)`), or move the query into clax_fleet::coord as a function the CLI calls.","summary":"`clax-fleet status` hard-codes a 20-second member window instead of using clax_fleet::coord::MEMBER_TTL","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:57.772931Z","id":"5185bff9c66de80add196ef498b6a032db26577c76566f545b362b3820ff4782","body":{"detail":"main() sets up a JSON subscriber when asked (main.rs:225-232) but reports the error serve() returns (a bad catalog, CLAX_KEK missing, PostgreSQL unreachable) as plain `clax-fleet: ...` on stderr. A log pipeline that parses JSON lines drops or misfiles it, and it is the line an operator looks for when a pod crash-loops. The module docs (main.rs:4-8) also name the flag `--drain-delay`; clap derives `--drain-delay-secs` from the field (main.rs:186-187), as docs/kubernetes.md:378 says.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":238},"end":{"line":243},"content_hash":"fcff2e64e283ad41ae1dec30c7c83fc73a5c946c62bbf03cfe9476ba9d8e84f6"},"suggested_fix":"Log the error with tracing::error!(error = %format!(\"{e:#}\"), \"clax-fleet failed\") before exiting (keeping eprintln for check-catalog, whose output a person reads), and correct the flag's name in the module docs.","summary":"A fatal error is printed with eprintln even when CLAX_FLEET_LOG_FORMAT=json, so the one line explaining a crash loop is not JSON","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:07:36.178972Z","id":"a134f2656ff5e4f63ab3f8719a89b3ebdb2545afeb0d13281699fe353d43bfa0","body":{"detail":"Fence::check (pg.rs:479-500) locks the instance's clax_fleet_leases row FOR SHARE for the whole write transaction. PgCoordinator::renew_all (crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs:248-275) renews every lease the pod holds in one UPDATE of those same rows, and a non-key UPDATE takes FOR NO KEY UPDATE, which conflicts with FOR SHARE (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/explicit-locking.html#ROW-LOCK-CONFLICT-TABLE). So the renewal waits for every write transaction in flight on any of the pod's instances; a page commit of up to CLAX_FLEET_DB_MAX_TRANSACTION_MIB or a 64 MiB publish (crates/clax-core/src/publish.rs:11) on a loaded server may run up to the 120 s statement_timeout per statement (pg.rs:63). Fleet::tick awaits renew_all before members() and the next heartbeat (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:765-783), so while it waits the pod stops heartbeating (out after 20 s, docs/kubernetes.md) and the leases whose rows renew_all has not yet reached pass their 30 s LEASE_TTL (coord.rs:34). Other pods' acquire (coord.rs:216-245) takes those unlocked rows, and when the renewal returns it lacks them, so fleet.rs:841-845 stops the instances: one slow write on instance A moves unrelated instances B and C, exactly when the database is slow.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":479},"end":{"line":500},"content_hash":"d0ddae99b0e29caab0366d55cf5f6186e7f6365618474518dcf003bbc7372be3"},"suggested_fix":"Keep what a write fences on apart from what renewal updates: e.g. a clax_fleet_fences (instance PRIMARY KEY, holder, epoch) row that Fence::check locks FOR SHARE and only acquire rewrites, with expires left in clax_fleet_leases for renew_all; or have Fence::check lock FOR KEY SHARE and make takeover change a key (a fence row keyed by (instance, epoch) that acquire deletes). Also give renew_all a lock_timeout (SET LOCAL lock_timeout) shorter than LEASE_TTL so a tick never stalls behind data I/O.","summary":"Every fenced write holds its lease row FOR SHARE, so one slow write blocks the pod's single renew_all UPDATE and can cost the pod its other instances' leases","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:07:36.179062Z","id":"306f9ff60f5a988d4dac8582c103bf5df332d10567ac35217d6c2006376ae796","body":{"detail":"Every write transaction locks its lease row FOR SHARE first (Fence::check, pg.rs:479-500) and then sends further statements (pages.rs:185-229, content.rs:128-146). If the pod's node dies or is partitioned between those statements (no FIN or RST reaches the server), the server backend sits idle in transaction holding the row lock. The new owner's acquire (crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs:216-245, INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE on that row) waits on it, fails at the 120 s statement_timeout, and is retried every tick, until the server's own TCP keepalive closes the session: tcp_keepalives_idle defaults to the OS (7200 s idle on Linux) and idle_in_transaction_session_timeout defaults to 0, off (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-client.html#GUC-IDLE-IN-TRANSACTION-SESSION-TIMEOUT). A node failure, the case the leases exist for, can keep each instance that was writing down for over two hours. config() sets only client-side keepalives and statement_timeout (pg.rs:157-163); docs/kubernetes.md (Connections) does not mention the server side.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":157},"end":{"line":163},"content_hash":"fd81f9e49bfb2be069463bf4ee4b8e6fd368296c2c559a499ef07b6d2b7e23c1"},"suggested_fix":"Add `-c idle_in_transaction_session_timeout=<a few seconds, below LEASE_TTL>` (and `-c tcp_keepalives_idle=10 -c tcp_keepalives_interval=5 -c tcp_keepalives_count=3`) to the options config() sends, since no write transaction waits on the client between statements; document the server settings for PgBouncer deployments, where startup options do not reach the server.","summary":"A pod lost mid-transaction leaves its server session idle in transaction holding the lease row FOR SHARE, so the instance cannot be taken over until the server notices the dead peer","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:07:36.179102Z","id":"7d819944676052c679a4e8237929975f9cf431b4c57a767756d4e5278acd84e4","body":{"detail":"config() sets keepalives(true) and keepalives_idle(30 s) only. tokio-postgres leaves keepalives_interval and keepalives_retries to the OS (tokio-postgres 0.7 Config::keepalives_interval/keepalives_retries), which on Linux is 75 s x 9 probes, so an idle connection to a silently lost server is noticed after about 30 + 675 s. Keepalives are sent only while the connection is idle: a job that has sent a statement (a commit's bytea batch) and awaits its acknowledgement is governed by TCP retransmission (tcp_retries2, about 15 minutes on Linux) and statement_timeout is enforced by the server, which is the party lost. Meanwhile the job occupies one of the pool's threads (pg.rs:195-219) and its caller blocks (Pool::run). docs/kubernetes.md (Connections) says the keepalives and statement_timeout make a database lost without a reset fail calls instead of hanging them, and the closed record 869880ef claimed the same.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":157},"end":{"line":163},"content_hash":"fd81f9e49bfb2be069463bf4ee4b8e6fd368296c2c559a499ef07b6d2b7e23c1"},"suggested_fix":"Also set keepalives_interval (e.g. 5 s), keepalives_retries (e.g. 3) and tcp_user_timeout (e.g. 30 s, which bounds unacknowledged sends) in config(), all available on tokio_postgres::Config, and state the resulting bound in docs/kubernetes.md.","summary":"Client keepalives start after 30 s but use the OS probe interval and count, and do not cover a connection with unacknowledged data, so a lost server hangs calls for minutes, not the bound docs/kubernetes.md implies","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:07:36.179133Z","id":"b6ed90ff768ed0b1ca21d7862549fb68eaaf256884ecb9e096adb62e83bda72f","body":{"detail":"tokio-postgres Config::options assigns, it does not append (tokio-postgres 0.7.18 src/config.rs:319-322), and config() calls it after parsing the URL (pg.rs:134-136, 160-163). A deployment whose CLAX_POSTGRES_URL carries `options=-c search_path=clax` (a schema per fleet in a shared database) or `-c lock_timeout=...` loses it without a word; with search_path dropped, migrate creates the clax_fleet_* tables in public instead. The URL is otherwise honoured (sslmode is checked against CLAX_POSTGRES_TLS, pg.rs:137-156), so this one silent override is surprising.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":157},"end":{"line":163},"content_hash":"fd81f9e49bfb2be069463bf4ee4b8e6fd368296c2c559a499ef07b6d2b7e23c1"},"suggested_fix":"Append to the URL's options: read c.get_options(), and set `<given> -c statement_timeout=<ms>` (keeping a statement_timeout the URL sets, or refusing it as the sslmode check does); add a case to tls_on_never_falls_back_to_plain_text's neighbour in crates/clax-fleet/tests/postgres.rs.","summary":"config() replaces any `options` the PostgreSQL URL gives with its own statement_timeout, silently","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:07:36.179160Z","id":"0e3ea12b75ecf688ccf13e0bdbb9dd2594fd1857512c9524cc3d046ff30ed817","body":{"detail":"config() sends `options=-c statement_timeout=120000` in every startup packet (pg.rs:160-163). PgBouncer accepts only the startup parameters it tracks and answers others with an error unless they are listed in ignore_startup_parameters (https://www.pgbouncer.org/config.html#ignore_startup_parameters), and listing options there discards it. docs/kubernetes.md (Connections) says 'Alternatively, put PgBouncer in transaction mode in front (the fleet keeps no session state beyond a transaction)' without either caveat: an operator following it gets pods that cannot connect, or, after the usual workaround, no statement_timeout at all, which the same paragraph relies on to fail calls on a lost database.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":157},"end":{"line":163},"content_hash":"fd81f9e49bfb2be069463bf4ee4b8e6fd368296c2c559a499ef07b6d2b7e23c1"},"suggested_fix":"Send the timeout inside each transaction instead (`SET LOCAL statement_timeout` as the first statement of the fenced transactions and reads, or one batch with BEGIN), or document that behind PgBouncer the role must carry it (`ALTER ROLE clax SET statement_timeout = '120s'`) and options must be listed in ignore_startup_parameters; add the caveat to docs/kubernetes.md.","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md offers PgBouncer in transaction mode, but every connection sends an `options` startup parameter, which PgBouncer refuses by default and drops when told to ignore it","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:07:36.179189Z","id":"2c3fb8b97aeef2aeed222af63413694f375c126718fcbc19b1c85cd30794aaeb","body":{"detail":"config() sets no application_name (pg.rs:157-163), so every clax-fleet session shows an empty application name in pg_stat_activity and the server log. The pool names its threads clax-pg-<name>-<i> (pg.rs:201) and the process knows its pod (POD_NAME), but nothing of that reaches the server. Diagnosing the lock waits on clax_fleet_leases (Fence::check FOR SHARE, pg.rs:479-500) or a long transaction means telling the data pool, the coordinator pool and the status command apart, and which pod holds a session.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":157},"end":{"line":163},"content_hash":"fd81f9e49bfb2be069463bf4ee4b8e6fd368296c2c559a499ef07b6d2b7e23c1"},"suggested_fix":"Unless the URL gives one, set application_name to `clax-fleet/<pod>/<pool name>` in Pool::connect or config() (tokio_postgres::Config::application_name).","summary":"Set application_name on every connection so an operator can tell the fleet's sessions, and which pod and pool each is, in pg_stat_activity","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:07:36.179215Z","id":"3707dfe87794bc99cc2a7ad5d5ec2d387a1046097140c632e60cecb8d8211881","body":{"detail":"tls_connector always loads webpki_roots::TLS_SERVER_ROOTS (pg.rs:103-104) and then adds the CA file's certificates. A deployment that names its database's private CA still accepts any certificate a public CA issues for that host name, unlike libpq, where sslrootcert names the only roots (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/libpq-ssl.html). pem_slice_iter yields nothing for a file without a CERTIFICATE block (a DER file, a key, an empty mount), so a wrong CLAX_POSTGRES_CA starts without error and the first connection fails with rustls' unknown-issuer error, naming neither the file nor its emptiness.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":99},"end":{"line":123},"content_hash":"245839ed9e2de6c7a94ed3712ae34d3c927254ba0959e361edf6e80bdf45d626"},"suggested_fix":"When ca_file is given, trust only its certificates (or offer CLAX_POSTGRES_CA_ONLY / document the union explicitly in docs/kubernetes.md, TLS), and fail in tls_connector when the file yields no certificate, naming the path.","summary":"CLAX_POSTGRES_CA adds to the public roots rather than replacing them, and a file with no PEM certificate in it is accepted silently","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:07:36.179242Z","id":"f89d2fe57ed3c5acb697b4233b73c983bdeb982897d497bf10458ad4028ddb1c","body":{"detail":"migrate fails when the stored version exceeds SCHEMA_VERSION (pg.rs:423-427). Version 2 (pg.rs:389-400) only changes storage parameters and adds two tables a version 1 binary never reads, yet a version 1 binary started against it (a rollback, or an old pod restarting mid-rollout) exits at start. docs/kubernetes.md (Upgrading) rolls back by restoring a dump, losing every write since; for the fleet's own schema that cost is avoidable whenever a migration is additive.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":316},"end":{"line":317},"content_hash":"894fe03aba9b3f0f2a5679874168436141b16beab77b382d671476515f4f7f65"},"suggested_fix":"Store a second number beside the version, the oldest build that can still run on it (`compatible`), raised only by migrations that change what older builds read or write; migrate refuses only when this build's SCHEMA_VERSION is below `compatible`.","summary":"migrate refuses any newer schema, so an additive migration such as version 2 still keeps the previous release from starting on a migrated database","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:07:36.179272Z","id":"61896764c46049a7fb9dfcf727408f4057ac4ee8a587113d874ce982a962ebdd","body":{"detail":"Pool::send pushes every job onto an unbounded mpsc channel (pg.rs:191, 252-255) and Pool::run blocks its caller until a thread takes it, with no limit on the wait. When the server slows, the data pool's CLAX_POSTGRES_POOL threads (8 by default) are all busy and every app data call (spawn_blocking, app_data.rs:165), page read and publish piles up behind them, each holding a blocked thread, while the admin port's metrics (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:1001-1067) show requests and lease losses but neither queue depth, wait time nor connection failures. Sizing CLAX_POSTGRES_POOL (docs/kubernetes.md, Connections) has nothing to go on.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":229},"end":{"line":279},"content_hash":"e6c995af1390026c7b31f3310f0aef45b5e094187ec2f738e91c2edf8c6b0cf1"},"suggested_fix":"Count queued and running jobs, a wait-time histogram (or sum and count) and reconnects per pool in Pool, and export them in /metrics as clax_fleet_pg_*; optionally fail a job that waited past a bound instead of queuing without limit.","summary":"The pool's queue is unbounded and unmeasured: /metrics shows nothing of jobs waiting for a PostgreSQL thread, how long they waited, or reconnects","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/content.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:07:36.179316Z","id":"d9290dfde493764a35f6db4c0ff7d79b7693b4af6597da0d173428156a6facf6","body":{"detail":"read_file and read_asset check the cache, release its lock, query PostgreSQL, then put what they read (content.rs:342-365, 534-557). put_asset, delete_asset and remove_version commit and then forget the key (content.rs:528-530, 574-575, 466-467). A read that queried before the write committed and puts after its forget leaves the old bytes cached: a clip written again (the comment at content.rs:528 says this happens) keeps showing its old image, a deleted asset stays downloadable from this pod, and a version removed and written again at the same number serves the removed version's file. Nothing else invalidates the entry; it lives until 64 MiB of later reads push it out (CACHE_BYTES, content.rs:36), which on a quiet instance may be never. The store's content lock covers changes only (module docs, content.rs:9-12), not reads.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":341},"end":{"line":366},"content_hash":"3abde5e5d1f8aba858fe1c2456f683f3ad364180393447fc3933ff9029af14fc"},"suggested_fix":"Give Cache a generation per artifact (a HashMap<String, u64> bumped by forget); a read notes the generation before its query and puts only if it is unchanged afterwards. Add a unit test that interleaves get-miss, forget, put.","summary":"A read in flight when an asset is rewritten or deleted, or a version removed, puts the old bytes into the cache after the write's forget, and they are served until evicted","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/app_data.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:07:36.179354Z","id":"e02a90144d5bd0c765e7ad0383730bdd6d5a7d90f367a21e0c680846a8984f7f","body":{"detail":"Stores::erase takes the entry out of the map (app_data.rs:148), then waits on its state lock for a call in progress (149-153), then deletes the file's rows (154-156). A call that reaches Stores::entry after the removal gets a new Closed entry (83-95), finds the file still present (pages.size, 123-127), opens it and runs its op; once erase deletes the rows, that open store still holds the file in the VFS, so its next commit writes only its dirty chunks and, if the size changed, a clax_fleet_files row: `app/<artifact ID>` exists again, partial, or as orphan chunks with no file row that nothing deletes. The window spans erase's wait for an in-progress call, which may be an import. MovingAppData::call reaches the host this way when the artifact is deleted after a call's state check (crates/clax-http/src/app_data_host.rs:213-232, 287-296). The fix for record 482b3979 (State::Erased) covers only a call that took the old entry.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":145},"end":{"line":157},"content_hash":"2f224f9f7f3260e866ec88dd4bbdd18622f3d66a914f98a1f9d73d1f2002b6b5"},"suggested_fix":"Leave the entry in the map, marked Erased under its lock, until delete returns, then remove it only if the map still holds that same Arc (Arc::ptr_eq); later calls then wait on it and answer NotFound, and calls after its removal find no file. Add a test that starts a call while erase waits.","summary":"erase removes the artifact's entry before it waits and deletes, so a call arriving in that window opens a fresh entry on the file being deleted and later commits into it","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:47.862637Z","id":"1a2dceec0c7ee6fe463aef12dbe55f42dfd01379b44d0b09b6f480d3625068fa","body":{"detail":"PgCoordinator::renew_all's UPDATE matches on instance, epoch and holder only (coord.rs:258-262), and MemCoordinator::renew_all the same (coord.rs:422-429); neither checks `expires > now()`. release() marks a lease free by setting expires = now() (coord.rs:281-283), keeping holder and epoch, so a renewal at the same epoch still matches it afterwards. crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:426-430 keeps the ticker running while Fleet::drain stops every instance (\"The ticks go on renewing the leases of what still runs while the instances stop\"): Fleet::renew_all (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:814-833) reads the holds, skipping only slots already marked stopping, then awaits coord.renew_all. A stop that sets `stopping` after that read and releases before the UPDATE runs (the coordinator pool is slow or queued) gets its lease revived. The post-processing then skips the instance (`running` is None), so nothing releases it again: for up to LEASE_TTL (30 s) Fleet::route on every other pod sees a live lease naming the drained pod and forwards there (fleet.rs:480-486), which is the outage release() exists to avoid in a rolling update. The contract (coord.rs:478-491) does not say whether an expired or released lease may be renewed.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":248},"end":{"line":274},"content_hash":"f547943c20637bd555fb37497baddffc8a26127a8ac200525f151c2dc0b9c782"},"suggested_fix":"Add `AND l.expires > now()` to renew_all's WHERE, and `*expires > now` to MemCoordinator::renew_all; a holder whose lease expired then sees it lost and stops, which the epoch fence already makes safe. Add to contract::run: after release(&inst, &a, e), renew(&inst, &a, e) answers false and lease() stays None.","summary":"renew_all extends a lease whatever its expiry, so a renewal that lands after release() or release_all() makes the released lease live again for LEASE_TTL","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","correctness","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:47.862706Z","id":"00a5ce221dccf02f0d276ad82a5bad241ffc0c00027902ff0068319444ff5b39","body":{"detail":"heartbeat resets `joined` only when the row's `seen` is older than MEMBER_TTL (coord.rs:143-145; MemCoordinator the same at coord.rs:358-361). A member is keyed by pod name alone, and a container the kubelet restarts in place keeps the pod's name and IP (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:920-922 says so). A restart, including the first CrashLoopBackOff restarts, heartbeats again within 20 s, so the new process inherits the old run's age. Fleet::tick's handover (fleet.rs:786-797) moves instances from live owners to a preferred member once `p.age >= coord::SETTLE`, so they move to the restarted process at once. SETTLE's own doc (coord.rs:35-38) says it exists 'so a process that starts and stops at once moves nothing'. A crash-looping pod therefore keeps pulling its rendezvous share away from healthy pods, and each crash leaves those instances unavailable until it restarts or their leases expire.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":132},"end":{"line":156},"content_hash":"7150134a77162caf7acebb7824e1b391fae4dd2381a301ebadd34f7e54d8cf62"},"suggested_fix":"Give each process a run ID (random at start) and send it with heartbeat: store it in clax_fleet_members (a schema step in crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs) and reset `joined` when it differs from the stored one, as well as after a lapse. Mirror it in MemCoordinator and add a contract check that a heartbeat from a new run restarts the age.","summary":"A container restarted in place keeps its member row's `joined`, so SETTLE does not hold instances back from a process that has just started (or is crash-looping)","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:47.862746Z","id":"4816460a061e2be32032509e22125a58ed26274acca0333f8bbdcdc1dade3391","body":{"detail":"Member has only pod, addr and age (coord.rs:40-49), and preferred() scores every live member alike (coord.rs:106-112). docs/kubernetes.md, Upgrading (lines 314-319), says instances migrate when they first start on a new pod and that 'Migrated data cannot be read by the older binary, which refuses a newer schema'. deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml:9-13 rolls with maxSurge 1, so old and new pods are members together. Rendezvous hashing sends a member's instances to the best remaining member when it leaves. If a new pod leaves before the rollout ends (OOM kill, failed liveness probe, node drain, an autoscaler scale-down), each instance it migrated goes to whichever member scores highest, possibly an old pod. There Fleet::open fails and the lease is released (fleet.rs:591-601), and the instance answers 'did not start' on every request until no old pod is preferred. Each request repeats the acquire, the database open and the failed migration check. kubectl rollout undo hits the same failure for every migrated instance, which the docs only cover as 'restore the dump'.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":40},"end":{"line":112},"content_hash":"cf9ea2dfd2d2c230d6a003cbdd1870651598d2bae58e77d2b9726cd6f80c0446"},"suggested_fix":"Record each member's schema/build version in the heartbeat (a column in clax_fleet_members) and have preferred() consider only members at the highest version present (or at least at the instance's recorded version, stored when its database migrates), so a migrated instance never lands on an older binary. Make the start failure for 'database is newer than this binary' log the version gap and back off instead of retrying on every request.","summary":"Placement ignores binary version: during a rollout an instance a new pod has migrated can be placed on an old pod, which refuses the newer schema, and stays down until the rollout ends","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","devops","upgrades","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:47.862923Z","id":"deaf728b78de6112cb1404a0e70c1c7168e5977119a0afbf9ba1dbd22022b7b1","body":{"detail":"contract::run's doc (coord.rs:459-463) leaves 'the rules about expiry' to MemCoordinator's tests (coord.rs:560-606), and crates/clax-fleet/tests/postgres.rs:190-195 runs only contract::run against PostgreSQL. PostgreSQL has a separate implementation of every expiry rule: acquire's `WHERE clax_fleet_leases.expires <= now()` (coord.rs:232), lease()'s `expires > now()` (coord.rs:203), members()' and heartbeat's make_interval comparisons (coord.rs:144, 179), and release_all's filter (coord.rs:302). None of them is exercised, so a boundary or interval mistake there (one that lets two pods hold a lease, or never frees one) passes CI. A clock need not be injected: the PostgreSQL test can age rows directly.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":459},"end":{"line":521},"content_hash":"faac0600c77992e7461cb7706bc34c6a1e33bdcb6a36cddc9f86c2e89e024f95"},"suggested_fix":"Add a second contract function taking an `age(instance_or_pod, Duration)` hook: MemCoordinator implements it with advance(), and the PostgreSQL test with `UPDATE clax_fleet_leases SET expires = expires - $2 WHERE instance = $1` (and the same on members.seen). Then run the expiry cases (acquire after expiry raises the epoch, an expired lease reads as none, a lapsed member's age restarts) against both coordinators.","summary":"The expiry rules, the core of fencing, are tested only on MemCoordinator; PgCoordinator's own expiry SQL (expires <= now(), the joined reset) never runs in a test","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","tests","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:48.817726Z","id":"b81c6ae19227fe4698ba2f47b06a27c207c02db822fed30d76558dc0ebae8b79","body":{"detail":"At bac1e8702, crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs:123-135 (Proxy::new) sets only set_connect_timeout(CONNECT_TIMEOUT), set_nodelay, pool_idle_timeout(IDLE_TIMEOUT=60s, proxy.rs:102), pool_max_idle_per_host; `grep -n 'keepalive\\|timeout' proxy.rs` shows no keepalive and no other timeout except the upgrade connect (proxy.rs:203). proxy.pool.request(...).await (proxy.rs:190), handshake (proxy.rs:209) and send_request (proxy.rs:219) are unbounded. Fleet::handle forgets the route only on 502/503 (fleet.rs:399-407); cached_route keeps the address for ROUTE_CACHE=10s (fleet.rs:88, 355-358). main.rs and fleet.rs add no request timeout layer (grep -in timeout: only CONNECTIONS_DRAIN and STOP_TIMEOUT). Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"922988b532974b7741e44fa7d86ae06fcabb139ea7b1ad11a8ab1ca5a8eedfeb","summary":"confirmed: forwards have no timeout past connect and pooled connections no TCP keepalive","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:48.818006Z","id":"47c30e26e264334d167b836c403ab304701612eb53519f1b0eb0ba6390e2f07e","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:1085-1087: allowed() is `peer.ip().to_canonical().is_loopback() || f.is_peer(headers)`, guarding /status (fleet.rs:1101-1111) and /metrics (1113-1129); the admin port listens on 0.0.0.0:7481 (docs/kubernetes.md:365). docs/kubernetes.md:263 recommends a mesh with mTLS, and docs/kubernetes.md:275-277 promises /status and /metrics answer only to loopback or the peer key. Istio's sidecar delivers inbound traffic to the app from 127.0.0.6 (https://istio.io/latest/blog/2021/upcoming-networking-changes/), which is in 127.0.0.0/8, so the check passes for every caller the NetworkPolicy (deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml, port 7481 rule) admits. Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"d0fd8aa8d5046d0f7ec4884036d4d817bad45041925e390d296cf55c401853e0","summary":"confirmed: admin allowed() trusts any loopback peer address, which a sidecar mesh makes every caller","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:48.818249Z","id":"89f5dd810ea016ad7ace6b37dcd441bfbf14e26c9525486b71cf99d7f58d93c5","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml admits 7481 from namespaces labelled clax.empathic.dev/metrics=true 'for a Prometheus that scrapes /metrics'. crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:1113-1129 answers 403 unless allowed() (fleet.rs:1085-1087: loopback or Fleet::is_peer, the x-clax-fleet-key peer key). docs/kubernetes.md:338-346 (Operating it) and 275-277 name no scrape credential. The same is_peer gates believing x-clax-fleet-hop / x-clax-fleet-instance in dispatch (fleet.rs:1199-1219), so the only working credential also makes the scraper a trusted forwarder on 7480. Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"5a51b34f14988b29ceb439b2ca6f73795c95da296aea341876dec4b27d41424a","summary":"confirmed: a Prometheus admitted to 7481 gets 403 on /metrics unless given the peer key","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:48.818484Z","id":"231061025e0a5bbb85f65b1abec9a96db958fc82972791018cf0e6041db9f51f","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:535-601 (Fleet::start): on open() Err it bumps start_failures, logs tracing::error!, loses the fence, releases the lease and returns Unavailable; nothing is stored on the slot (`*running` stays None), so the next caller holding slot.running (fleet.rs:537) re-runs coord.acquire (561-563) and open(). open() (fleet.rs:606-698) on PostgreSQL runs pg::claim_origin, clax_pagevfs::open_with and Store::open_on (migrations) in spawn_blocking each time; on shards a missing home bails at fleet.rs:613-619 and place_kek refuses a mismatched KEK (fleet.rs:625-627, 1133+). Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"0d1e30d07e119b8d1310bddf6ab9ece529eaaf8d17fddbc50b63052f7be67d39","summary":"confirmed: a failed start is retried in full on every request, with no backoff or remembered failure","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:48.818721Z","id":"c65adf525f2165cd0b0bac72eee5c818bf4cea5f7d7eb3da9e97802fd01e7a9b","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-core/src/config.rs:729-731 defaults setup_secret_env to SETUP_SECRET_ENV = \"CLAX_SETUP_SECRET\" (config.rs:1290-1291). `grep -n setup_secret crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs` returns nothing: Catalog::parse (catalog.rs:196-250) checks repeated public origins (authorities set) and site-domain nesting, not setup_secret_env. fleet.rs open() reads each instance's variable from the one process environment (hosted.setup_open(), fleet.rs:641-647). docs/kubernetes.md:108 uses CLAX_SETUP_SECRET_ACME by convention only. Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"42b00a21d3ce34ff74eddb0165d7a3e3360b3a3ff48b69479022814cafb29674","summary":"confirmed: Catalog::parse lets instances share a setup_secret_env, defaulting all to CLAX_SETUP_SECRET","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:48.818952Z","id":"34e910382b99c1ccc53f27d427d6d626eb4bca1fd37f415d8eb6e6a0f25e4845","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs:26-29 and docs/kubernetes.md:116 state 'each tenant's pages stay cross-site from every other tenant'; the check at catalog.rs:226-250 is at_or_under label nesting only. docs/kubernetes.md:106-107 pairs public_origin acme.clax.example.com with site_domain acme-sites.example.com: both have registrable domain example.com under the Public Suffix List (https://publicsuffix.org/), so they are same-site, which docs/hosted/public-suffix.md:1-20 describes (Domain= cookies, SameSite not separating). Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"4d874951d338e2426f9920c4f949d84cf672a68b5de1e4e14efae99c6e20e60d","summary":"confirmed: the nesting check does not make tenants cross-site, and the documented example shares example.com","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:48.819187Z","id":"c48f152be47c84b011e8a7ea461eb1555ff7a90542f021d33129562f368213f2","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:784-799: on n % 3 == 2 every running instance whose coord::preferred member is another pod aged >= coord::SETTLE is pushed to stops, with no cap; fleet.rs:802-808 joins all stops with futures::future::join_all, each bounded by STOP_TIMEOUT (fleet.rs:91, 753). deploy/k8s/postgres/hpa.yaml:13-14 allows 2 to 10 replicas. docs/kubernetes.md:228-234 slows scale-down because 'each move cuts that instance's open streams' but describes no pacing for scale-up. Suggestion is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"c5566b466dc866b81afa99d60726c086bb09c67f17f6bf3ce57b3eb5d6d61cd7","summary":"confirmed: a scale-up hands over every preferred instance in one tick, unpaced","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:14.037559Z","id":"79767583f0d4de8326f5cf0b86d503b9e692554adc6355146293e97fa3526113","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:979 `\"commit\": clax_core::build_commit()`; crates/clax-core/src/lib.rs:61-71 returns \"unknown\" until set_build_commit fills the OnceLock. `grep -rn 'set_build_commit' crates` (excluding .qual) hits only crates/clax-worker/src/instance.rs:668, crates/clax-commands/src/lib.rs:72 (inside clax_commands::run, which clax-fleet never calls) and clax-core's definition. crates/clax-fleet has no build.rs (ls: BUILD.bazel Cargo.toml src tests), main() (main.rs:221-243) never sets it, .dockerignore excludes .git, and deploy/Dockerfile passes no CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT. docs/kubernetes.md:341-342 promises \"its version and commit\".","kind":"comment","references":"ad7b26f31a4ee3b39e16beb15aff3f2b51d8f900783c6f0b2deead1435dfaf2d","summary":"confirmed: nothing in clax-fleet calls set_build_commit, so /status's commit is always \"unknown\"","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:41.331370Z","id":"84adc9c1a0c2d9d697e503fb1ed06c09aaf9433b57a724db7319a36749f028d4","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:499-525: client built with `Client::builder(TokioExecutor::new()).build_http()` (no connect or request timeout); on Ok(resp) it parses the body with `serde_json::from_slice(&body).unwrap_or_default()` without checking resp.status(), then prints `shard {shard} ({pod or \"?\"}): ready {v[\"ready\"]}, running {v[\"running\"]}`. The 403 from fleet.rs:1107/1120 (`StatusCode::FORBIDDEN.into_response()`, empty body) parses to Null, printing \"ready null, running null\".","kind":"comment","references":"b0081557f5efda61823d9080d42793e70b1738766fa0849d15382b591b1dc731","summary":"confirmed: the SQLite status loop ignores the response status and the client has no timeout","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:41.331659Z","id":"e2a9f0b637124dee9044739fb398c80a82016d289a21acd7de1d1470da398867","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:453 `ON m.pod = l.holder AND m.seen >= now() - interval '20 seconds'`; crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs:30 `pub const MEMBER_TTL: Duration = Duration::from_secs(20);`. The two agree today but nothing ties them.","kind":"comment","references":"0fac0089023241a46b7c3b14e01f794347f82d0de406cd1e24ae00cf28a964fd","summary":"confirmed: status hard-codes interval '20 seconds', duplicating coord::MEMBER_TTL","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:41.331907Z","id":"baa36d301732a1922fb13eab7ca990c10fc14adfec03e4918abfc345e1973338","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:225-232 installs a JSON subscriber for LogFormat::Json; main.rs:238-241 handles Err(e) with `eprintln!(\"clax-fleet: {e:#}\")`, not tracing. main.rs:6 says `--drain-delay`, while the field is `drain_delay_secs` (main.rs:186-187, clap long derives --drain-delay-secs), matching docs/kubernetes.md:378 `--drain-delay-secs`.","kind":"comment","references":"5185bff9c66de80add196ef498b6a032db26577c76566f545b362b3820ff4782","summary":"confirmed: the fatal error goes through eprintln regardless of --log-format json, and the module docs name --drain-delay","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:53.481299Z","id":"708e80eddc3c16c382c390392b80f8587c489733b97c03e64ce69c6d990f5f57","body":{"detail":"coord.rs:258-262 (PgCoordinator::renew_all WHERE l.instance = h.instance AND l.epoch = h.epoch AND l.holder = $1, no expires test) and coord.rs:422-429 (MemCoordinator, holder and epoch only), while release sets expires = now() keeping holder and epoch (coord.rs:281-283, 437-443). Fleet::renew_all (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:814-833) collects holds when `stopping` is false and try_lock succeeds, then awaits coord.renew_all with no lock held; Fleet::stop (fleet.rs:718-758) sets stopping, takes `running`, stops the core and calls coord.release in that window. The post-processing at fleet.rs:835-840 skips the slot once `running` is None, so nothing releases the revived lease. Fleet::route on another pod then sees a live lease with holder != its pod and forwards (fleet.rs:480-486). main.rs:428-430 keeps the ticker running during fleet.drain(). contract::run (coord.rs:467-520) never renews after a release.","kind":"comment","references":"1a2dceec0c7ee6fe463aef12dbe55f42dfd01379b44d0b09b6f480d3625068fa","summary":"confirmed: renew_all ignores expiry, so a renewal racing a stop's release revives the released lease","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:53.481600Z","id":"5dab58c00c9a676a1a60aff3abb3f25b158c33833f33ead0a79212662132198c","body":{"detail":"PgCoordinator::heartbeat resets joined only WHEN clax_fleet_members.seen < now() - MEMBER_TTL (coord.rs:143-145); MemCoordinator keeps joined while now - seen <= MEMBER_TTL (coord.rs:358-361). Members are keyed by pod alone (ON CONFLICT (pod), coord.rs:140). fleet.rs:920-922 states a container restarted in place keeps its pod's name. A crash does not run Fleet::drain, so leave() never deletes the row. Fleet::tick hands instances to a preferred member once p.age >= coord::SETTLE (fleet.rs:785-797), against SETTLE's doc (coord.rs:35-38).","kind":"comment","references":"00a5ce221dccf02f0d276ad82a5bad241ffc0c00027902ff0068319444ff5b39","summary":"confirmed: joined resets only after a MEMBER_TTL lapse, so an in-place container restart keeps its age past SETTLE","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:53.481846Z","id":"0c772e64b9cd10aed4facc83b4b46d9ee1c099fafb0080eb324ba42b4c0af5f2","body":{"detail":"Member carries only pod, addr, age (coord.rs:40-49); preferred() ranks all members by rendezvous score alone (coord.rs:106-112). docs/kubernetes.md:313-318 says instances migrate on first start on a new pod and the older binary refuses a newer schema (crates/clax-store/src/lib.rs:810). deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml:9-13 rolls with maxSurge 1, maxUnavailable 0, so old and new pods are members together. A start failure releases the lease and answers 'did not start' (fleet.rs:592-600), with nothing that keeps the next request from retrying the same start.","kind":"comment","references":"4816460a061e2be32032509e22125a58ed26274acca0333f8bbdcdc1dade3391","summary":"confirmed: placement has no version, so a migrated instance can be placed on an older pod that refuses its schema","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:53.482819Z","id":"432d341e7ed05fbfe928f961b0e642becf0dba21795f70790a66a1a22fce4ef4","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/tests/postgres.rs:190-195 runs only contract::run (and is #[ignore] without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL); contract::run's doc leaves expiry to MemCoordinator's tests (coord.rs:459-463, tests at 560-606). One correction to the detail: acquire's `expires <= now()` (coord.rs:232) and lease()'s `expires > now()` (coord.rs:203) do run against PostgreSQL, at the boundary release() makes (expires = now(), coord.rs:282), in contract::run at coord.rs:486-488 and 509-511. What never runs is any lapse of time: the make_interval TTLs of members() and heartbeat's joined reset (coord.rs:144, 179) and a lease expiring by LEASE_TTL. The suggestion stands for those.","kind":"comment","references":"deaf728b78de6112cb1404a0e70c1c7168e5977119a0afbf9ba1dbd22022b7b1","summary":"confirmed: PgCoordinator's TTL arithmetic is untested; only the expires = now() edge runs, through release","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:11:19.316348Z","id":"5ff40fb6601889cec1c88335f28cc03e12c4970e1f9625009a05d533a7e51617","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:479-500 (Fence::check) runs SELECT ... FROM clax_fleet_leases ... FOR SHARE inside each write transaction, held to commit (e.g. crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs:185-229, several bytea batch statements after the check). crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs:248-275 renews every hold in one UPDATE clax_fleet_leases ... FROM unnest(...), which takes FOR NO KEY UPDATE row locks; the PostgreSQL conflict table (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/explicit-locking.html#ROW-LOCK-CONFLICT-TABLE) lists FOR NO KEY UPDATE as conflicting with FOR SHARE. The statement's other renewals are not visible until it commits. Fleet::tick (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:762-809) awaits self.renew_all before coord.members() and returns before the next tick's heartbeat; fleet.rs:841-845 stops every instance missing from the renewed set ('its lease was lost'). LEASE_TTL is 30 s and MEMBER_TTL 20 s (coord.rs:30-34), STATEMENT_TIMEOUT 120 s (pg.rs:63). Kind concern fits: the outcome needs a write slower than the TTLs.","kind":"comment","references":"a134f2656ff5e4f63ab3f8719a89b3ebdb2545afeb0d13281699fe353d43bfa0","summary":"confirmed: renew_all's single UPDATE waits on any write holding a lease row FOR SHARE, and the tick stalls with it","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:11:19.316626Z","id":"c1fa880044368791e024e392715087cdbb2d57aeca50db826a6653ae8175d37f","body":{"detail":"Fence::check takes FOR SHARE first (crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:479-500) and the transaction then sends more statements (crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs:186-229: chunk batches, files row, truncate, commit). acquire's INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE on that row (crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs:216-245) must wait for the row lock. config() (pg.rs:157-163) sets only client-side connect_timeout, keepalives, keepalives_idle and the statement_timeout option; statement_timeout does not apply to a session idle in transaction, idle_in_transaction_session_timeout defaults to 0 (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-client.html#GUC-IDLE-IN-TRANSACTION-SESSION-TIMEOUT), and server tcp_keepalives_idle 0 means the OS default (7200 s on Linux, https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-connection.html#GUC-TCP-KEEPALIVES-IDLE). `grep -n -i idle_in docs/kubernetes.md crates/clax-fleet/src/*.rs` finds nothing.","kind":"comment","references":"306f9ff60f5a988d4dac8582c103bf5df332d10567ac35217d6c2006376ae796","summary":"confirmed: a session left idle in transaction holds the lease row FOR SHARE until the server drops it, and nothing bounds that","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:11:19.316865Z","id":"0bc4ceb513ab8ec5d1ce9d8612ba92a6eca0926ebbb496cf54c636468348e6cc","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:157-159 sets keepalives(true) and keepalives_idle(30 s) only. tokio-postgres 0.7.18 (the version in Cargo.lock) defaults keepalive_config.interval and .retries to None and tcp_user_timeout to None (src/config.rs:261-268), and src/keepalive.rs:21-33 sets interval/retries on the socket only when Some, so the OS values apply (Linux tcp_keepalive_intvl 75 s, tcp_keepalive_probes 9). Keepalive probes are not sent while data is unacknowledged; that case is bounded by retransmission (tcp_retries2) unless tcp_user_timeout is set, which Config::tcp_user_timeout offers (src/config.rs:152). statement_timeout is enforced server-side. docs/kubernetes.md:207-209 says keepalives plus the 120 s statement_timeout make a database lost without a reset 'fail calls instead of hanging them'.","kind":"comment","references":"7d819944676052c679a4e8237929975f9cf431b4c57a767756d4e5278acd84e4","summary":"confirmed: probe interval, count and TCP_USER_TIMEOUT are left unset, so detection of a lost server is OS-bound, not 30 s","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:11:19.317102Z","id":"f63946fcce2b34be20eb5ee2ae88502acc2655a2bdf63dad29541a0933abf159","body":{"detail":"tokio-postgres 0.7.18 src/config.rs:319-322: `pub fn options(&mut self, options: impl Into<String>) -> &mut Config { self.options = Some(options.into()); self }` assigns. crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:135-137 parses the URL, then pg.rs:160-163 calls .options(\"-c statement_timeout=...\"), so a URL `options=-c search_path=...` is dropped with no message. migrate's DDL (pg.rs SCHEMA_1/SCHEMA_2) names its tables unqualified, so they land in the server's default search_path.","kind":"comment","references":"b6ed90ff768ed0b1ca21d7862549fb68eaaf256884ecb9e096adb62e83bda72f","summary":"confirmed: config() overwrites the URL's options with its statement_timeout","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:11:19.317339Z","id":"15abd64de84b2d69ad814279d9d740fa85eeca01bc264b38f7f575d233e5c981","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:160-163 always sends the options startup parameter. https://www.pgbouncer.org/config.html#ignore_startup_parameters: 'By default, PgBouncer allows only parameters it can keep track of in startup packets ... All other parameters will raise an error'; listing a parameter there makes PgBouncer ignore it. That page's track_extra_parameters section gives a third route (parameters inside options may be tracked, so statement_timeout could be tracked and kept), which the docs would need to name. docs/kubernetes.md:205-209 says 'Alternatively, put PgBouncer in transaction mode in front' and relies on the statement_timeout in the same paragraph, with neither caveat.","kind":"comment","references":"0e3ea12b75ecf688ccf13e0bdbb9dd2594fd1857512c9524cc3d046ff30ed817","summary":"confirmed: every startup packet carries options=-c statement_timeout, which PgBouncer rejects by default, and docs/kubernetes.md offers PgBouncer without the caveat","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:11:19.317575Z","id":"900fbcab0d69daadbb016c030bc5b28003f05fef825a662bda8b429cb626355e","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:157-163 sets connect_timeout, keepalives, keepalives_idle and options only; tokio-postgres 0.7.18 defaults application_name to None (src/config.rs:254) and offers Config::application_name (src/config.rs:330-331). Pool threads are named clax-pg-<name>-<i> (pg.rs:201) but nothing passes that name to the server. A URL that names application_name keeps it, so this is an operator-convenience suggestion, as recorded.","kind":"comment","references":"2c3fb8b97aeef2aeed222af63413694f375c126718fcbc19b1c85cd30794aaeb","summary":"confirmed: no application_name is set, so the fleet's sessions are anonymous in pg_stat_activity","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:11:19.317815Z","id":"2843b1dca80173fe05af7a9294cc3c1114b401c7b1d590a4ea91c2101963533d","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:103-113: RootCertStore::empty(), then roots.extend(webpki_roots::TLS_SERVER_ROOTS...), then each cert from CertificateDer::pem_slice_iter(&pem) is added. rustls-pki-types 1.15.1 (Cargo.lock) src/pem.rs ends the iterator on Ok(None) ('no PEM section to read', pem.rs:133, 163, 214) rather than erroring, so a key-only, DER or empty file adds nothing and the connector builds. libpq's sslrootcert replaces the trust roots (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/libpq-ssl.html). docs/kubernetes.md:197-199 describes the CA only as a supplement when public roots do not cover it, so suggestion is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"3707dfe87794bc99cc2a7ad5d5ec2d387a1046097140c632e60cecb8d8211881","summary":"confirmed: CLAX_POSTGRES_CA is added to the webpki roots, and a file with no CERTIFICATE section loads zero certificates without error","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:11:19.318050Z","id":"7de348e9148c76f5a30ad3560f4ab49132b8b044469d8de716f3908a07f1e253","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:423-427 returns 'the database has the fleet's schema {version}, newer than this build's' whenever version > SCHEMA_VERSION. SCHEMA_2 (pg.rs:389-400) only sets storage parameters on clax_fleet_chunks and creates clax_fleet_instances and clax_fleet_settings. docs/kubernetes.md:314-318 (Upgrading) says the older binary 'refuses a newer schema' and rolling back means restoring the dump. Suggestion is the right kind: it is a rollback-cost improvement, not a defect.","kind":"comment","references":"f89d2fe57ed3c5acb697b4233b73c983bdeb982897d497bf10458ad4028ddb1c","summary":"confirmed: migrate refuses any stored version above SCHEMA_VERSION, including the additive version 2","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:11:19.318296Z","id":"9129e6ad00dfbd9432106ea0a7da54b9fa1ccaf1fd518fc6073a897296d12675","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:191 builds the queue with mpsc::channel::<Job>() (unbounded); Pool::run (pg.rs:269-278) blocks on rx.recv() with no limit. crates/clax-fleet/src/app_data.rs:165 drives each app data call through spawn_blocking. The metrics in crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:995-1068 are requests_local/forwarded/unavailable, forward_errors, instance starts/start failures/stops, leases_lost, lease_renew_failures, handovers, catalog_reload_failures, heartbeat_failures and four gauges (instances_running, catalog_instances, catalog_stale, ready); none covers pool queue depth, wait time or reconnects.","kind":"comment","references":"61896764c46049a7fb9dfcf727408f4057ac4ee8a587113d874ce982a962ebdd","summary":"confirmed: the pool's job channel is unbounded and /metrics exports nothing about it","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/content.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:11:19.318537Z","id":"c306ae797723d30b72b927a61a1001d6cfac22e59b913c4fc318906d9f53eca4","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/content.rs:342-365 (read_file) and 534-557 (read_asset): cache get, lock released, query, then cache().put. put_asset forgets after its transaction (content.rs:528-530, with the comment 'A clip is written again over its old bytes'), delete_asset after its (574-575), remove_version after its (466-467). Store-side reads take no content lock: crates/clax-store/src/assets.rs:414-442 (read_asset_async/asset_bytes_async) call content.read_asset directly, and the module docs (content.rs:9-12) scope the lock to changes. One narrowing: a deleted asset fetched through Store::read_asset_async is gated by get_asset's row (assets.rs:419), so the 'stays downloadable' case applies to callers that read the content store directly (clips via read_clip, content/mod.rs:378); the rewritten-clip and reused-version cases stand as stated.","kind":"comment","references":"d9290dfde493764a35f6db4c0ff7d79b7693b4af6597da0d173428156a6facf6","summary":"confirmed: a read that queried before a write committed can put stale bytes after the write's forget; reads take no lock","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/app_data.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:11:19.318777Z","id":"c422b03a4362f98b6c654d77d553e3d25bdebd541e5bdafb097cf326eccf640d","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/app_data.rs:147-157: erase removes the entry from the map (148), then locks the removed entry's state and marks it Erased (149-153), then deletes the file (154-156). Stores::entry (82-95) inserts a new Closed entry for a missing key, and Stores::call (114-141) checks pages.size(&name) and opens the store on any existing file; the new entry's state mutex is independent of the one erase holds, so nothing orders it after the delete. The caller does not exclude it: MovingAppData::call (crates/clax-http/src/app_data_host.rs:287-295) checks state and then sends with no lock shared with erase (296-298), and move_rows itself calls inner.erase after an Import (227-230).","kind":"comment","references":"e02a90144d5bd0c765e7ad0383730bdd6d5a7d90f367a21e0c680846a8984f7f","summary":"confirmed: a call arriving between erase's map removal and its delete gets a fresh entry and opens the file still present","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/app_data.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:39.329092Z","id":"37690855e0c4321ed6d886e316f7f51a67adda08de357282335e013263e42c28","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"Stores::erase keeps the entry in the map, marked Erased under its lock, while it deletes, then removes it only if the map holds the same Arc (Arc::ptr_eq). Test: app_data::tests::a_call_during_an_erase_waits_and_finds_no_store. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"e02a90144d5bd0c765e7ad0383730bdd6d5a7d90f367a21e0c680846a8984f7f","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:39.410116Z","id":"0359b0167259d2f71dc58ed58e33aae330c2bcb66cc480f45e033ce2628d15dc","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"Catalog::parse refuses two instances with the same effective setup_secret_env (the default CLAX_SETUP_SECRET included), naming both, and refuses CLAX_KEK, CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY and CLAX_POSTGRES_URL as one. Test: catalog a_bad_catalog_is_refused_naming_what_is_wrong. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"42b00a21d3ce34ff74eddb0165d7a3e3360b3a3ff48b69479022814cafb29674","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:39.493242Z","id":"e362b14a8b62fb2aa8cefb0fdb0d9c229ff0f86899bd60f14941dbb1eea6829d","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"No public-suffix crate is in Cargo.lock, so the catalog module docs and docs/kubernetes.md now state the weaker guarantee (no nesting) and require each tenant's site domain to be its own registrable domain or a PSL entry; the example site domain is acme-sites.example.net. Test renamed no_site_domain_is_at_another_tenants_hosts. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"4d874951d338e2426f9920c4f949d84cf672a68b5de1e4e14efae99c6e20e60d","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/content.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:39.575400Z","id":"b30e942207b5120041fb1d032b3f333f2634b49373d9ad84717ff7f98597859d","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"The content cache counts forgets; a read that missed caches what it read only if no forget happened since (Cache::get returns Miss(count), put_read). Test: content::tests::a_read_across_a_forget_is_not_cached. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"d9290dfde493764a35f6db4c0ff7d79b7693b4af6597da0d173428156a6facf6","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:39.656292Z","id":"3406d30084386134b12060332ed1196f35af44155a71e7ee6184d209f7eaafaf","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"Members report the store schema their build migrates to and leases keep the highest schema an instance was opened at; preferred() and the handover consider only members at or above it, and route_leased answers 503 when no member can open it. Tests: leases::an_instance_never_goes_to_a_member_whose_build_cannot_open_it, coord::tests::an_instance_goes_only_to_a_member_that_can_open_its_data. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"4816460a061e2be32032509e22125a58ed26274acca0333f8bbdcdc1dade3391","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:39.737999Z","id":"7339a8bc48841bf054d39e3d5e98b452e68f68afbd8326a6b040698e331face6","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"Each Fleet sends a random run ID in its heartbeat; a heartbeat with a different run resets joined, in PgCoordinator and MemCoordinator. Test: coord::contract::expiry (a new run has not settled), on MemCoordinator; on PostgreSQL in the ignored the_postgresql_coordinator_keeps_the_contract. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"00a5ce221dccf02f0d276ad82a5bad241ffc0c00027902ff0068319444ff5b39","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:39.818682Z","id":"a0afc059e62f447f2c45e87b27683bb2809feb358a501248691d0ee3fec60edf","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"renew_all renews only unexpired leases (expires > now()), both coordinators. Test: coord::contract::run checks renewing after release and release_all answers false and the lease stays free. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"1a2dceec0c7ee6fe463aef12dbe55f42dfd01379b44d0b09b6f480d3625068fa","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:39.899969Z","id":"051e9efed62fba9776d57ee05157206bc19126cd7dc0b910a61d437159567488","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"New coord::contract::expiry with a Time hook, run on MemCoordinator (advance) and on PostgreSQL through PgTime ageing rows (crates/clax-fleet/tests/postgres.rs, ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL). Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"deaf728b78de6112cb1404a0e70c1c7168e5977119a0afbf9ba1dbd22022b7b1","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:39.981073Z","id":"8d3dc4918d752f115fe8ede5aba51509f3429358cf7d81b9dc976eb5f3ea2326","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"A failed open is recorded on the slot; requests answer 503 without a new start until a backoff (2 s doubling to 60 s) passes, reset on success or a catalog change; /status lists failing instances. Time comes from FleetConfig.clock. Test: shards::a_failed_start_is_retried_after_a_backoff_not_per_request. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"0d1e30d07e119b8d1310bddf6ab9ece529eaaf8d17fddbc50b63052f7be67d39","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:40.061828Z","id":"1eeb216ccdb0bd8a59bdf23c190fbef68bf7110aa5eb36ccedd606c91a71cebf","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"The handover moves at most HANDOVERS_PER_TICK (4) instances per tick and counts the rest in clax_fleet_handovers_deferred_total. Test: leases::a_handover_moves_a_few_instances_per_tick. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"c5566b466dc866b81afa99d60726c086bb09c67f17f6bf3ce57b3eb5d6d61cd7","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:40.142680Z","id":"2e976c8fa12c657c6a014f96bd6bf26e80a04d40f1dc16a5b178d4e3e1ca2005","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"Port 7481 no longer trusts loopback peers: /status there needs the peer key. A loopback-only listener (CLAX_FLEET_LOCAL_ADMIN_LISTEN, default 127.0.0.1:7482, non-loopback refused) serves /status open. Test: shards::the_admin_port_asks_for_the_key_for_status_alone. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"d0fd8aa8d5046d0f7ec4884036d4d817bad45041925e390d296cf55c401853e0","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:40.223600Z","id":"116228b8d7f3d7ea3f051bba0cad42dd48862936b28edb794fab98d4cce7128a","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"/metrics asks for no credential (counters and gauges only; base/networkpolicy.yaml limits who reaches 7481), so a scraper never needs the peer key; docs/kubernetes.md gains a Scraping /metrics part with a PodMonitor. Test: shards::the_admin_port_asks_for_the_key_for_status_alone. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"5a51b34f14988b29ceb439b2ca6f73795c95da296aea341876dec4b27d41424a","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
